{"event":"answer","q":"How does volcanic ash affect my health?","lang":"en","alerts":[],"retrieved":[{"id":93,"source":"kantei-funka","score":0.0172,"cos":0.764},{"id":92,"source":"kantei-funka","score":0.0169,"cos":0.763},{"id":94,"source":"kantei-funka","score":0.0166,"cos":0.704}],"a":"Contact with volcanic ash can cause skin inflammation, hurting or swelling, and germs may enter through scratches [1]. Volcanic ash can go deep into the lungs, causing coughing, shortness of breath, runny nose, phlegm, and chest tightness for people with certain conditions like asthma [2]. If it gets in your eyes, they may feel gritty, itch, hurt, become red, or cause conjunctivitis [2].","cited":[1,2],"invalid_citations":[],"grounded":true,"admits_gap":false,"no_source":false,"ttft_ms":350,"total_ms":1041,"input_tokens":765,"output_tokens":84,"tokens_per_s":120.1,"rss_mb":3977}
